# The Mod

The Emissary’s Daughter is a custom-voiced Skyrim follower mod, bringing an in-depth, lifelike character to a game that isn’t designed for it. 

**The project goals are:**

- Provide an intellectually interesting character, different than other followers by providing depth of character and narrative  
- This character will \*not\* assume a “completed” state \- “time in service” and quest progression will not be a substitute for engaging with this character as a person (This is not a “waifu” mod. Romance \- or even friendship \- is *not* guaranteed; she *can* and *will leave* some players)  
- The character will react to \- and *change narrative trajectory* \- based on player *behavior* not quests or dialogue choices

# Narrative Contract

“Alenawe is a politically intelligent, emotionally guarded companion whose arc explores institutional loyalty under stress, without requiring romance or dependency.”

Once this is satisfied, **no additional cleverness is required**.

**Anything that doesn’t serve that contract is out of scope, even if it’s cool.**

If you ever find yourself thinking:

“This would be interesting…”

That is not a valid criterion.

The only valid question is:

“Does this sharpen or dilute the contract?”

If it dilutes, it’s not just unnecessary — it’s harmful.

# Exit Condition:

“When Alenawe can accompany a full playthrough without missing reactions, breaking immersion, or contradicting her worldview, the project is complete.”

“Done” checklist:

**Every major questline: reacts at least once (no more than 3\) (react: One voiced line OR one short exchange OR one conditional comment)**  
**Every major faction: has a stance (no more than two)**  
**Every common activity loop (travel, combat, cities): has ambient presence (no more than 5\)**  
**2 “Major” narrative quests**  
**2 “Minor” narrative quests (single-location, single-theme)**  
**Complete affinity system (negative, neutral, warm, love are stable states)**  
**Narrative closure state (arc complete)**

# Theme/Lessons

Is the player deciding her fate, or is she? (Agency)  
Narratively:  
 • It resists player power fantasy  
 • It preserves her agency  
 • It creates friction without antagonism

Mechanically:  
 • Explains affinity caps  
 • Justifies disagreement  
 • Supports non-romance endings

This question should **never be answered cleanly**. If it is, you’ve broken the character.

The *best* outcome is ambiguity:

The player influences conditions. She chooses responses.

What survives when institutional conditioning collides with lived reality? (belief under pressure)  
centers on:  
 • What breaks  
 • What adapts  
 • What hardens

It also avoids presuming the answer is “conditioning fails.” Sometimes it *wins*. That’s more interesting.

This theme should be **answered provisionally**, not conclusively.

What must you believe for power to make sense — and what happens when you stop believing it? (self-deception as survival)  
It shows up in:  
 • What she rationalizes  
 • What she refuses to discuss  
 • What she reframes as “necessary”  
 • What she avoids examining too closely

You don’t need answers. You need *tells*.

For example (not text, just structure):  
 • Early: power is procedural, abstract, justified  
 • Mid: power is regrettable but necessary  
 • Late: power is survivable only if partially ignored

Or she never resolves it — and that’s fine.

# Writing goals:

**You don’t need her to comment on everything. You need her to comment often enough to feel alive.**

**A practical rule that works well for companions:**

**• Every major questline: reacts at least once**  
 **• Every major faction: has a stance**  
 **• Every common activity loop (travel, combat, cities): has ambient presence**

**Once each bucket is filled, stop.**

**Do not chase coverage. Coverage is infinite.**

**No-Revision Line:**  
**\-No Dialogue re-writes. We’ve decided on a tone, themes and personality. Everything else is organic.**

**\-lean in to the flaw to drive progression/conflict**  
**\-at odds with the world and knows it**  
**\-talk like something is fete accompli**  
**\-worldview is sharply defined \- will it break, wear down, or refine?**  
**\-immediate action, no waiting for player. Decisions (good or bad) uniquely hers**  
**\-unique voice**  
**\-characters move events, events dont move characters (add suspense back)**  
**\-need more than just one line of dialogue responses to events \- let decisions *cost her something***  
**\-decisions/actions that surprise herself \- something unexpected, but inevitable in retrospect**  
**\-treats PC as she would like to be treated (Mordin giving advice, reminiscing without judgement)**  
**\-let judgements reveal fears/wounds/hopes. Transform from faux authoritative to respect to options \- and let the PC push back**

